<Layout title="About me" description="I’m Viswaroop, a staff software engineer interested in platforms, identity, and ideas that survive contact with real life.">
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow blue">The notebook / The person behind it</p><h1>Hello, I’m Viswaroop.</h1><p>I build platforms. I care about the boundaries between things. And I write to understand what I’ve learned.</p></header>
<div class="page-body two-col"><div class="prose"><p>I’m a Staff Software Engineer focused on platform and identity systems. I’ve spent years running multi-tenant Kubernetes platforms across AWS and GCP, replacing managed identity services with open-source IAM, and making GitOps work as teams grow.</p><p>The parts I find interesting are rarely contained in a single tool. They show up between teams, between authentication and authorization, and between an architecture diagram and the system people actually operate.</p><h2>What I’m working through</h2><p>Lately, I’ve been exploring what those boundaries mean for autonomous agents: scoped identity, isolated execution, and workflows that act on behalf of people. I’m keeping that exploration separate from the systems I’ve already built. Both belong in the notebook.</p><h2>Beyond the terminal</h2><p>Reading is another way I work through ideas. In my 2025 notes, I tracked what I applied rather than how many passages I highlighted—from taking ownership during incidents to creating calmer environments at home.</p><p>I’m interested in the ideas that cross over: how a book on leadership changes an incident review, or how a lesson about focus changes the way I build.</p><p><a href="/blog/2026-01-05-books-i-read-in-2025/">These are the books, and the ideas, that stayed with me.</a></p><h2>Why this space exists</h2><p>Building something is only half the work. Explaining the decisions, the failure modes, and what I’d reconsider makes the learning useful to someone else. That’s what I want this site to hold.</p></div>
